{"id":"~Xiaoyu_Huang1","name":"Xiaoyu Huang","counts":{"accepted_papers":1,"first_position_papers":0,"co_first_author_papers":null,"corresponding_author_papers":null,"Oral":1},"papers":[{"id":"acslhrig33","title":"Flow Policy Gradients for Robot Control","abstract":"Likelihood-based policy gradient methods are the dominant approach for training robot control policies from rewards. These methods traditionally rely on differentiable action likelihoods, which constrain policy outputs to simple distributions like Gaussians. In this work, we show how the recently proposed flow matching policy gradient framework—which bypasses likelihood computation entirely—can be made practical for training more expressive robot control policies, both from scratch and by fine-tuning. We introduce an improved objective that enables success in legged locomotion, humanoid motion tracking, and manipulation tasks, as well as robust sim-to-real transfer on two humanoid robots. We then present ablations and analysis on training dynamics. Results show how policies can exploit the flow representation for exploration when training from scratch, as well as improved finetuning robustness over baselines.","summary":null,"type":"Oral","topics":["强化学习与奖励","生成策略与扩散","世界模型与仿真","移动、全身与人形","导航与探索","安全、泛化与鲁棒性"],"title_topics":[],"authors":["~Brent_Yi1","~Hongsuk_Choi1","~Himanshu_Gaurav_Singh1","~Xiaoyu_Huang1","~Takara_E._Truong1","~Carmelo_Sferrazza1","~Yi_Ma4","~Rocky_Duan1","~Pieter_Abbeel2","~Guanya_Shi1","~Karen_Liu1","~Angjoo_Kanazawa1"],"institutions":["Amazon","Carnegie Mellon University","Stanford University","University of California, Berkeley","University of Hong Kong"],"pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.02481","openreview":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=acslhrig33","project":["https://hongsukchoi.github.io/fpo-control"],"code":["https://github.com/amazon-far/fpo-control"],"poster_date":"2026-11-10","poster_time":"5:00–6:30 PM","poster_venue":"JW Marriott","poster_number":63,"hardware":[],"has